#!/usr/bin/env python3

r"""Prepare the runtime failure observer's CI evidence before AI analysis.

Purpose
-------
This is the deterministic collection and preparation phase, not the investigator.
It selects a bounded set of dotnet/runtime CI builds and packages their relevant
AzDO/Helix evidence and consumed XHarness source revisions for offline analysis.
The later agent decides whether failures suggest XHarness fixes and reads live
GitHub issues/PRs for context and deduplication. This script neither performs that
investigation nor edits source files, creates PRs, or collects issue/PR data.

Processing steps
----------------
1. Fix a UTC scan time and query the main-branch builds for each definition in
   DEFINITIONS. From a bounded list of failed/partially-succeeded builds, select
   the latest failure within seven days and up to five preceding failures.
   History builds may be older than seven days; there are at most 54 selected
   builds across the nine definitions.
2. Prepare selected build metadata through hlx, then traverse complete timelines.
   Read only successful Helix submission logs to discover job IDs. Enumerate
   each job's complete work-item inventory, skipping passing work items and
   negative Helix service exits before requesting full failure consoles.
3. Parse XHarness invocation boundaries, terminal exit codes, version SHAs, and
   diagnostic signatures. Alias expansion must not create duplicate invocations;
   child-tool exits must not be mistaken for XHarness exits. Retain evidence
   identities and console line ranges alongside normalized commands/signatures.
4. Compute recurrence within each definition using the exact normalized
   (XHarness exit code, command, signature) tuple. Analysis requires an exit in
   IMPROVEMENT_EXIT_CODES and matches in two distinct preceding history builds.
   Mark history-only, weak, out-of-scope, and duplicate source tuples as skips;
   keep them in the full index rather than silently discarding them.
5. Resolve consumed XHarness revisions for source improvement candidates:
   prefer the console's informational-version SHA, otherwise fetch runtime's
   eng/Version.Details.xml at the exact build revision. Ensure the starting and
   consumed revisions and bounded history are available, then package them in a
   validated bare repository. Fetches may update Git objects/metadata, but the
   supplied checkout's HEAD and working files are not changed.
6. Export and validate the isolated hlx cache as an offline snapshot, then write
   the handoff files. Explicit retention/policy skips are nonfatal; missing
   required evidence, ambiguous parsing, source preparation, or snapshot errors
   make the scan incomplete. Snapshot finalization and diagnostic output are
   still attempted after a collection failure.

Invocation
----------
Run from the XHarness repository root with Python 3, git, and an installed hlx
executable matching HLX_VERSION (currently 0.11.0):

    python3 .github/workflows/runtime-failure-observer-collect \
        --output-dir /tmp/observer-collection \
        --xharness-repo "$PWD" \
        --hlx /path/to/hlx \
        --now 2026-10-06T12:00:00Z

--output-dir must not already exist, even as an empty directory. --xharness-repo
must be the checkout root, not a subdirectory. --now is optional RFC3339 input;
without it, the CLI captures the current UTC time once. A fixed clock fixes the
selection policy, not the contents of live services. The workflow installs and
verifies the hlx package before invoking this script.

Outputs and completion
----------------------
All paths below are relative to --output-dir; JSON results are files, not stdout:

    observer-manifest.json  Policy/clock, selection, full traversal inventories,
                            provenance, counts, skips/errors, snapshot/source
                            validation, and output hashes.
    failure-index.json      All parsed invocations from failed work-item
                            consoles, separate Helix/XHarness exit codes,
                            recurrence, analysis decisions, and evidence refs.
    analysis-inputs.json    Compact starting input for the agent: manifest
                            projection, eligible source tuples, one witness per
                            matching history build, and aggregated skip counts.
    filed.tsv               Empty ledger for the agent's same-run PR tracking.
    hlx-snapshot/           Validated raw CI evidence for hlx offline MCP replay
                            via HLX_EVAL_SNAPSHOT.
    source/xharness.git     Bare Git package of starting/consumed revisions and
                            history; not the editable workflow checkout.
    version-details/       Exact runtime version files, when fallback resolution
                            was needed, with hashes recorded in the manifest.
    hlx-manifests/          Per-build provider acquisition diagnostics.
    xdg-cache/hlx/public/   Isolated provider cache; retain for failed-export
                            diagnostics, not as a substitute for the snapshot.

Schema version 1 is used for the JSON handoff. Exit 0 means a complete scan;
exit 2 means a recorded incomplete scan; exit 1 indicates a startup error.
Argument validation also uses exit 2 and may produce no files. Consumers must
check manifest.complete/status/errors and required outputs, not just the exit
code. manifest.empty is true only for a complete scan with no source exits in
the improvement table; it does not simply mean analysis-inputs has no eligible
tuples. The workflow's runtime-failure-observer-handoff.py validates this contract
and emits noop/missing_data; the collector itself emits neither safe output.

Implementation map and tests
----------------------------
ObserverCollector.run orchestrates the phases; select_builds and
parse_xharness_invocations implement selection/parsing; build_analysis_inputs
creates the compact projection. HlxV011Provider uses hlx CLI and MCP calls against
one shared isolated cache. GitHubVersionDetailsResolver and GitSourcePreparer
handle exact-revision metadata and source/history preparation. EvidenceProvider,
VersionResolver, and SourcePreparer are injectable boundaries for deterministic
unit tests; the CLI wires their live implementations. See
tests/test_runtime_failure_observer_collect.py beside the workflow and
docs/runtime-observer.md for operational/replay guidance.
"""

